  "summary": "Gun-Nac is Compile’s vertically scrolling NES shooter, set in an artificial planetary system where a mysterious force has brought ordinary objects to hostile life. The premise allows an eccentric procession of enemies across eight stages. Beneath that playful appearance sits a generous weapon system: five primary guns, four kinds of bomb and several levels of upgrades. The player continually chooses what to collect and what to keep, making the strength and shape of the ship’s attack a central part of surviving the next wave.",
  "body": [
    "Gun-Nac is Compile’s vertically scrolling NES shooter, set in an artificial planetary system where a mysterious force has brought ordinary objects to hostile life. The premise allows an eccentric procession of enemies across eight stages. Beneath that playful appearance sits a generous weapon system: five primary guns, four kinds of bomb and several levels of upgrades. The player continually chooses what to collect and what to keep, making the strength and shape of the ship’s attack a central part of surviving the next wave.",
    "Numbered pickups select the main gun, with options including a straightforward shot, a flamethrower and a guided boomerang. Primary fire has unlimited ammunition, while lettered pickups supply a separate stock of bombs. Repeatedly collecting the same type improves its level, so chasing every visible icon can be less useful than preserving a preferred weapon. Bomb upgrades also increase ammunition consumption. The system creates a small decision inside the scrolling action: whether a pickup will strengthen the current plan or replace it with something less suitable.",
    "Money collected during stages can be spent on upgrades in the shops between them. A wing pickup enlarges the ship and allows it to survive an additional hit, while also supporting stronger weapon upgrades. Those systems give a successful stretch of shooting consequences beyond the score display: surviving and collecting well helps prepare the next departure. Gun-Nac keeps that preparation within a brisk arcade rhythm, balancing the pleasure of increasingly powerful attacks with the need to read enemy movement and protect the ship carrying them."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "A strange fleet overhead",
    "Build the weapon you want",
    "A shop between the storms"
  ],
